.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-2-Clause
.Dd September 14, 2026
.Dt BSDNOTIFY 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm BSDNotify
.Nd capability-mode publish, subscribe, and timer service
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
implements the Notify interface, version 2.0.0, from
.Pa /Capabilities/System/Notify.cap ,
exposed as two endpoints, or tiers:
.Bl -tag -width "system.Notify.System"
.It Dq system.Notify
The open tier.
Resolvable by every session, including default user shells.
By default it may subscribe to any topic and publish only under
.Dq user.* ;
timers and state under other topics are refused.
.It Dq system.Notify.System
The gated tier.
The unit manifest declares it with
.Ql requires = [\(dqsystem.notify.system\(dq] ,
so
.Xr switchboard 8
resolves it only for callers whose anointment set covers that name; everyone
else receives
.Er ENOENT
before the provider is reached.
By default it may publish under any topic, set state, and use timers.
.El
.Pp
Which tier a session is on is decided by the endpoint it was accepted on
(cross-checked against the endpoint name switchboard resolved), never by
anything the client sends.
Operations a tier's policy does not permit fail with
.Er EACCES
and are audited.
.Pp
A system-scoped router owns the topic index, bounded client queues, and
monotonic
.Xr kqueue 2
timers.
Ephemeral publications carry optional bounded opaque data.
State publications retain one 64-bit value and monotonically increasing
generation per topic for the lifetime of a random router epoch.
The provider admits each global service connection to one fixed,
event-driven router worker over an unnamed capability channel.
No process or private ring is allocated per client.
The router permits at most one outstanding long-poll request per client while
continuing to dispatch unrelated client channels.
Concurrent requests receive
.Er EBUSY .
Client death therefore closes the router endpoint immediately, including
while an infinite event wait is pending, and the router removes the session,
subscriptions, and timers.
The provider and router remain in their own coalition, so one consumer exit
cannot
terminate notification delivery for other services.
.Pp
Subscriptions are exact topic matches.
Publisher identity comes from the authenticated component bootstrap and
cannot be supplied by the publisher.
Queues, topic count, timer count, topic length, and payload size are bounded.
A full subscriber queue drops the newest event for that subscriber and
increments its drop counter without blocking unrelated subscribers.
The next read also receives an explicit gap event with the loss count.
Queues are volatile.
A publish reply confirms router processing and loss accounting, not durable
subscriber delivery; events are not replayed after restart.
.Sh SECURITY
The router receives session endpoints only from the provider over its private
capability channel.
Consumer channels and router endpoints are non-transferable and locked
against fork and exec propagation.
The router cannot create sockets, fork, exec, receive ambient
.Dv SCM_RIGHTS
descriptors, or use ambient filesystem authority.
Its sole descriptor admission path is the pre-created capability channel;
each admitted session consumes the last hop of a linear transfer budget and
arrives non-transferable.
Subscription, publication, timer, bootstrap, and rejection decisions are
available through OpenBSM audit records and DTrace probes.
.Pp
Authority is loaded before sandbox entry from the bundle's
.Pa Config/BSDNotify.conf ,
delivered as a descriptor by
.Xr switchboard 8 .
The policy must be a regular file owned by root or the daemon's effective
user, must not be group- or world-writable, is opened without following
symbolic links, and must not exceed 64 KiB or contain embedded NUL bytes.
Applications cannot grant themselves publish, subscribe, state, or timer
authority; the router enforces the tier policy before dispatching a request.
A session holding
.Dv SERVICE_RIGHTS_ADMIN ,
which switchboard mints only onto an ambient root login session, bypasses the
policy on either tier.
Subscribers should also validate the authenticated publisher identity carried
in each event when that identity is relevant to application policy.
.Sh CONFIGURATION
.Pa BSDNotify.conf
is a UCL document with three optional top-level blocks; any other key is a
hard error and the daemon refuses to start.
A missing block takes the compiled-in default, which the shipped file states
explicitly.
.Bl -tag -width "system_default"
.It Ic default
The policy for every session on the open tier
.Dq system.Notify .
Default:
.Ql subscribe = [\(dq*\(dq]; publish = [\(dquser.*\(dq]; timers = false .
.It Ic system_default
The policy for a session on the gated tier
.Dq system.Notify.System
whose label has no
.Ic clients
entry.
Default:
.Ql publish = [\(dq*\(dq]; subscribe = [\(dq*\(dq]; timers = true .
.It Ic clients
Per-label narrowing of the gated tier, keyed by the immutable switchboard
manifest label.
An entry replaces
.Ic system_default
for that label on
.Dq system.Notify.System
only; it is never consulted for a session on the open tier.
Default: empty.
.El
.Pp
Each block holds
.Ic publish
and
.Ic subscribe
topic lists and a
.Ic timers
boolean.
A missing list or a missing
.Ic timers
denies; an empty block therefore denies everything.
.Ic subscribe
also governs state reads,
.Ic publish
also governs state writes and clears.
A topic list entry is one of:
.Bl -tag -width "prefix.*"
.It Ar topic
An exact topic, matching only itself.
.It Ar prefix Ns .*
A prefix pattern.
.Ql user.*
matches
.Ql user.x
and
.Ql user.x.y
but neither
.Ql user
nor
.Ql users.x .
The prefix must itself be a valid topic; patterns such as
.Ql *.x ,
.Ql user.*.y
and
.Ql user*
are rejected.
.It Li *
Every topic.
It must be the only entry in its list.
.El
.Pp
Example, narrowing one publisher on the gated tier:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
clients {
	"com.example/publisher" {
		publish = [ "system.shutdown.*" ];
		subscribe = [ "*" ];
		timers = true;
	}
}
.Ed
.Pp
.Xr notifyctl 8
.Cm configtest
validates a file with the daemon's parser.
.Sh DTRACE PROBES
The
.Dq BSDNotify
provider exports
.Sy session-start ,
.Sy session-end ,
.Sy session-admit ,
.Sy tier-policy ,
.Sy subscribe ,
.Sy publish ,
.Sy deliver ,
.Sy timer ,
.Sy list ,
and
.Sy reject .
.Pp
.Li session-admit(label, tier, rights, abi)
fires in the accept loop once a connection's identity has been checked
against the listener it arrived on
.Pq tier 0 open, 1 system ,
with the rights
.Xr switchboard 8
stamped and the client's ABI;
.Li tier-policy(label, tier, source)
fires in the router when the session's policy is chosen, naming the block
that answered:
.Dq default ,
.Dq system_default ,
or
.Dq clients .
A connection whose resolved endpoint disagrees with the listener it was
accepted on is refused before either fires and is recorded in the audit
trail
.Pq Dv AUE_BSDNOTIFY_POLICY , operation Li admit-tier-mismatch-* , result Er EPROTO ,
as every policy refusal is.
Probe arguments contain authenticated labels, topic names, bounded payload
lengths, operation identifiers, and result codes; notification payload bytes
are not exposed.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr kqueue 2 ,
.Xr libnotify 3 ,
.Xr libservice 3 ,
.Xr capsicum 4 ,
.Xr notifyctl 8 ,
.Xr switchboard 8
